course: dictionary
lesson: 8779
title: A word for a ruler
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: A shah is a title, a special name for a ruler. You will often see it with a name, as in «Shah Jahan», or with «the», as in «the Shah». Words from other languages can be hard, but the words around them give clues about who or what is being described.
explain: «The Taj Mahal in Agra, India, was built in the 1600s by the emperor Shah Jahan as a tomb for his wife Mumtaz Mahal, not as a palace to live in.» Shah Jahan is called an emperor, which is a ruler with great power, so shah must be linked to ruling. Try it: «built by the king Shah Jahan» still makes sense, so a shah is like a king or emperor.
explain: Shah is also a common family name, like Smith or Jones. So when you see Shah as a name with no clue about rulers, it is probably a person's family name. Context decides which meaning is right.
fact: A shah is a title for a ruler, like a king or emperor.
fact: «The Shah» was the king of Persia, now called Iran, in the past.
fact: Some rulers in Afghanistan and in old India also used the title shah.
fact: Shah is also a common family name in South Asia.
fact: The chess words "check" and "checkmate" come from the Persian "shah mat", which means the king is helpless.
exercise: «The Taj Mahal in Agra, India, was built in the 1600s by the emperor Shah Jahan.» Which word tells you Shah Jahan was a ruler? | *emperor | Taj | Agra | 1600s
exercise: In history lessons, «the Shah» was the ruler of which country? | Egypt | Scotland | *Persia, now called Iran | Japan
exercise: «The Taj Mahal was built as a tomb, not as a palace to live in.» What is a shah most like? | *A king or emperor | A kind of fruit | A tall tower | A school teacher
test: «Asha Shah is in our class.» In this sentence, what does Shah mean? | A tomb | A ruler's title | *A family name | A palace
test: «The Taj Mahal was built as a tomb for his wife, not as a palace to live in.» What was the Taj Mahal built as? | A palace to live in | A castle | *A tomb | A school
exam: In chess, "check" and "checkmate" come from Persian words meaning what about the king? | The king is asleep | The king is riding a horse | The king is very rich | *The king is helpless
